hinery. There are also the financial imperatives.\nThe ongoing push by the mining corporations to be more productive and more efficient is another powerful\ndriver in embracing automation technology.\n7\\� 2021 � 6 J3        13\nThe concept of a fully autonomous mine is a bit of a misleading term however. This is because the more\ntechnology is put into the field, the more people are needed to deploy, maintain and improve it. (20 ) The\n�����������������������!_!:����£]�� These include data scientists\nand engineers in automation and artificial intelligence. The mining companies claim automation and robotics\npresent opportunities to make mining more sustainable and safer. Employees will be offered a career that is\neven more fulfilling and more rewarding. ( 21 ) Workers' unions have                    the              of the\nintroduction of new technology, but they still have reservations about the rise of automation technology. Their\nmain concern is the potential impact on remote communities. As automation spreads further, the question is\nhow these remote communities will survive when the old jobs are eliminated. And this may well prove to be the\nmost significant impact of robotic technology in many places around the world.\nftJIJi��l:0tK�?�:•�·��it����. �;;���r����-\n19. What does the passage say about the mining                             �)®§���-*��mTM��I��&®\nindustry in Western Australia?                                         •*D �-��'*r�•*��m7�$�®\nD) UUJrl gm 1H.!l!f8¥ :ff! o li:U Cl 9) i:p tf!�J, 3t :jey, �R :7h ,       -��1IL ,��;ki A)    o\n§��$���:@��*r�o §��tt*                                          21. What is the attitude of workers' unions towards\n��mmn�•�5�®��••�•�mwA                                                 the introduction of new technology?\n*ro --��-BfflWa*��»-��-�                                           C) [ffitfr] gm\"Fmif8¥:lf!o  (21)i:ptf!�J,    Bt��\n§ �1.t1fifrr®mHft �.!lt��:;h D)\no                     o               