                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Directions: In this section, there is a passage with ten blanks. You are required to select one word for each blank from a list of choices given in a word bank following the passage. Read the passage through carefully before making your choices. Each choice in the bank is identified by a letter. Please mark the corresponding letter for each item on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre. You may not use any of the words in the bank more than once. A new study has drawn a bleak picture of cultural inclusiveness reflected in the children's literature available in Australia. Dr. Helen Adam from Edith Cowan University's School of Education "
                },
                {
                  "type": "blank",
                  "blankId": "cet6-paper-2021061-part-3-section-1-group-1-blank-26"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " the cultural diversity of children's books. She examined the books "
                },
                {
                  "type": "blank",
                  "blankId": "cet6-paper-2021061-part-3-section-1-group-1-blank-27"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " in the kindergarten rooms of four day-care centers in Western Australia. Just 18 percent of 2,413 books in the total collection contained any "
                },
                {
                  "type": "blank",
                  "blankId": "cet6-paper-2021061-part-3-section-1-group-1-blank-28"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " of non-white people. Minority cultures were often featured in stereotypical or tokenistic ways, for example, by "
                },
                {
                  "type": "blank",
                  "blankId": "cet6-paper-2021061-part-3-section-1-group-1-blank-29"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " Asian culture with chopsticks and traditional dress. Characters that did represent a minority culture usually had "
                },
                {
                  "type": "blank",
                  "blankId": "cet6-paper-2021061-part-3-section-1-group-1-blank-30"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " roles in the books. The main characters were mostly Caucasian. This causes concern as it can lead to an impression that whiteness is of greater value. Dr. Adam said children formed impressions about 'difference' and identity from a very young age. Evidence has shown they develop own-race "
                },
                {
                  "type": "blank",
                  "blankId": "cet6-paper-2021061-part-3-section-1-group-1-blank-31"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " from as young as three months of age. The books we share with young children can be a valuable opportunity to develop children's understanding of themselves and others. Books can also allow children to see diversity. They discover both similarities and differences between themselves and others. This can help develop understanding, acceptance and "
                },
                {
                  "type": "blank",
                  "blankId": "cet6-paper-2021061-part-3-section-1-group-1-blank-32"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " of diversity. Census data has shown Australians come from more than 200 countries. They speak over 300 languages at home. Additionally, Australians belong to more than 100 different religious groups. They also work in more than 1,000 different occupations. \"Australia is a multicultural society. The current "
                },
                {
                  "type": "blank",
                  "blankId": "cet6-paper-2021061-part-3-section-1-group-1-blank-33"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " promotion of white middle-class ideas and lifestyles risks "
                },
                {
                  "type": "blank",
                  "blankId": "cet6-paper-2021061-part-3-section-1-group-1-blank-34"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " children from minority groups. This can give white middle-class children a sense of "
                },
                {
                  "type": "blank",
                  "blankId": "cet6-paper-2021061-part-3-section-1-group-1-blank-35"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " or privilege,\" Dr. Adam said."
